1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Kajsa, (Karin, Cary, Svensdotter, Rasmussen) Ramseth died on homestead in Long Hollow Township, Roberts County, South Dakota, United States, near Sisseton about 1899 [after 6 June 1895 (1895 Census Minnesota Territ
2 CONC ory, Cary Ramseth wife of Ole Ramseth) and before 1900 (1900 US Federal Census, Ole a widower). In “Across the years - History of Sisseton”, states that Mother died even with the doctor’s care and is buried in Nels Peterson’s past
2 CONC ure, 3 years later Ramseth family moved to Canada. Immigration year to Canada of some family members 1902]. Family story is that Cary died from a lung/respiratory disease – possibly pneumonia or galloping consumption (tuberculosis
2 CONC ). Exact death date in U.S. is not known. No death or burial record has been found as of 2015. South Dakota death records don’t begin until 1905. Cary Ramseth is buried on the homestead land in Longhollow Township, Roberts County,
2 CONC  South Dakota according to information learned from a trip made in December 2014 and a return trip in September 2015. The grandson of Nels and Emma Peterson is the current owner of the homestead land in South Dakota. The legal lan
2 CONC d description for the pasture is: the east one-half of the southeast quarter of section 4, township 126 north, range 52 west of 5th P.M. in South Dakota, Roberts County, Long Hollow Township. Ole Rasmussen sold the lower 80 acres 
2 CONC to Nels Peterson and the upper 80 acres to David Lekness before moving to Saskachewan. Nels Peterson emigrated from Sweden in 1886. No known connection to Anna and Peter Petterson has been found. The Ramseth family likely moved to
2 CONC  this homestead in 1896 or 1897. The first record located in Roberts County of the Ramseth Family is 1897 school records. Ole became a widower with 5 children to support and raise in a new country not long after their arrival. Ole
2 CONC  never remarried.
